  },
  "articleBody": "When you're picking out new flooring for your bathroom, you'll hear terms like 'waterproof' and 'water-resistant' thrown around, and it's easy to get them confused. But there's a big difference! Water-resistant materials, like some laminates, can handle minor splashes and spills if you clean them up quickly. They'll resist water for a bit, but prolonged exposure can still cause damage. Think of it like a raincoat that can handle a light drizzle but might soak through in a downpour. \n\nWaterproof materials, on the other hand, are designed to completely repel water, no matter how much or how long it's exposed. This includes materials like ceramic or porcelain tile and luxury vinyl plank (LVP). These are your best bets for bathrooms where splashes, drips, and humidity are constant. With waterproof options, you get a much higher level of protection against leaks and moisture damage to your subfloor. It really comes down to your peace of mind and the level of moisture your bathroom typically experiences. For ultimate protection and durability in a high-moisture area, going truly waterproof is usually the smartest choice. If you’re unsure, give HH Remodeling LLC a call; we can help you pick the right stuff.",
